{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role in the history of the Crusader states during the twelfth century. His father was Raymond II who died under suspicious circumstances that many believed involved the Assassins yet young Raymond inherited a fragile territory surrounded by hostile Muslim powers. He spent much of his early reign stabilizing his county through diplomacy rather than constant warfare and this approach defined his political career throughout the decades.\n\n*Early Life and Succession*\nRaymond assumed power after the murder of his father and he immediately faced threats from Nur ad-Din who sought to expand Muslim control over the Levant. The new count negotiated a truce with Nur ad-Din to buy time for rebuilding his defenses and he also managed internal disputes within the nobility of Tripoli. His ability to navigate complex political landscapes allowed him to maintain independence while other Crusader lords struggled against overwhelming odds or fell victim to internal betrayals.\n\n*Regency and Political Influence*\nThe count became regent for Baldwin IV of Jerusalem when the young king showed signs of leprosy and this position gave Raymond immense influence over the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He acted as a moderating force against the aggressive factions led by Guy of Lusignan and Reynald of Châtillon who favored direct military confrontation with Saladin. Raymond advised caution and sought diplomatic solutions to avoid provoking a full-scale invasion but his counsel was often ignored by the more hawkish members of the royal court. His rivalry with Guy of Lusignan intensified over time and this division weakened the Christian response to the growing threat from the south.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nRaymond commanded a significant portion of the Christian army at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where Saladin achieved a decisive victory that shattered Crusader power in the region. Some contemporaries accused Raymond of treason because he withdrew his forces during the battle yet modern historians suggest he attempted a tactical retreat to save what he could of the army. The defeat led to the fall of Jerusalem and most other Crusader holdings but Raymond managed to escape to Tripoli where he died shortly after the catastrophe. His life represents the struggle of the Crusader states to survive amid rising Islamic unity and his diplomatic skills offered a different path that ultimately failed due to internal discord among the Latin Christians.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.9367987e-05}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.008417469}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.008035012}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.13125858}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.5762793e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -4.4107533e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -3.9339143e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.013786803}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.056518767}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.0060856966}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -2.9132013}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "